From: Brick Linux <brick@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] hush (NOMMU): fix crashes in $(...) started from a login shell

On NOMMU, hush re-executes itself for subshells and $(...) and passes the
parent's functions with "-F name body". Those functions point their name and
body into argv (body == NULL marks them). unset_func() knows not to free
them, but new_function() freed ->name unconditionally when a function was
redefined.

The re-executed child is started with the parent's argv0, so the child of a
login shell ("-sh") was itself a login shell and sourced /etc/profile again.
Any function defined there was redefined in every $(...) child, which freed a
pointer into argv and corrupted the heap: every command substitution crashed.

Fix both: don't free names of -F functions, and a re-executed child
("-$" option) is never a login shell.

--- a/shell/hush.c
+++ b/shell/hush.c
@@ -8292,11 +8292,12 @@ static struct function *new_function(char *name)
 		debug_printf_exec("func %p parent_cmd %p\n", funcp, cmd);
 		if (!cmd) {
 			debug_printf_exec("freeing & replacing function '%s'\n", funcp->name);
-			free(funcp->name);
-			/* Note: if !funcp->body, do not free body_as_string!
+			/* Note: if !funcp->body, do not free name or body_as_string!
 			 * This is a special case of "-F name body" function:
-			 * body_as_string was not malloced! */
+			 * they point into argv and were not malloced
+			 * (see unset_func). */
 			if (funcp->body) {
+				free(funcp->name);
 				free_pipe_list(funcp->body);
 # if !BB_MMU
 				free(funcp->body_as_string);
@@ -10425,6 +10426,9 @@ int hush_main(int argc, char **argv)
 		case '$': {
 			unsigned long long empty_trap_mask;
 
+			/* A re-executed subshell keeps argv0 ("-sh"), but it
+			 * must not run /etc/profile again. */
+			flags &= ~OPT_login;
 			G.root_pid = bb_strtou(optarg, &optarg, 16);
 			optarg++;
 			G.root_ppid = bb_strtou(optarg, &optarg, 16);
